# Pairs with RevisionMonotonic. The rollback deliberately stays INSIDE the
# declared 0..Cardinality(Txns) domain, so TypeOK still holds and the only
# property that can catch it is the monotonicity one. A mutation that also
# broke TypeOK would fire for the wrong reason and prove nothing about
# RevisionMonotonic.
MODULE: RevisionMonotonicRollback
TARGET: RevisionMonotonic
CLASS: Action
SUMMARY: decrements the registry revision within its declared domain, so it is no longer monotonic

--- FIND
Next ==
    \/ \E t \in Txns : PrepareTx(t)
--- REPLACE
\* MUTATION: the revision counter can go backwards, as it would if a stale
\* registry write were replayed over a newer one. Unfair, so it perturbs only
\* the safety property under test.
RevisionRollback ==
    /\ revision > 0
    /\ revision' = revision - 1
    /\ UNCHANGED <<phase, vote, decision, terminal, pend, orphanDone>>

Next ==
    \/ RevisionRollback
    \/ \E t \in Txns : PrepareTx(t)
--- END
